// Generated by `agent-api-kit guide` from the guide folder: edit the templates there, then run it again.
export const GUIDE_FILES: Record<string, string> = {
  "llms.txt": "# Yardhand\n\n> A self-hosted gateway: one endpoint for every tool behind it, from MCP servers and REST APIs its owner connected.\n> Find a tool by what you want to do, read its input schema, run it. Over MCP at `/mcp`, or over REST here.\n> Send `Authorization: Bearer <key>` on every call (one key per client), or an OAuth token the owner approves.\n\n## Start\n\n- MCP clients: connect to `/mcp`. You get `search_tools`, `describe_tool`, `call_tool`, `get_result`,\n  `list_upstreams`, `read_resource`, `get_playbook`, and any tools pinned for your key. The server instructions list\n  the upstreams you may use, and mark those with a manual. Playbooks are also listed as prompts (slash commands).\n- REST clients: `GET /api` links to everything. The usual path is three calls:\n  1. `GET /api/tools?q=<what you want to do>`: tools across every upstream, best match first.\n  2. `GET /api/tools/{name}`: the tool's description and JSON Schema input.\n  3. `POST /api/calls` with `{\"tool\": \"<name>\", \"arguments\": {…}}`, or `POST /api/tools/{name}/calls` with the\n     arguments as the whole body. The answer carries the tool's own result (`result.content`, and\n     `result.structuredContent` when it declares an output schema).\n- Typed clients: `GET /api/tools/openapi.json` describes every tool your key may use as an OpenAPI 3.1 operation.\n- The owner manages the gateway in the browser at `/app` (connections, keys, reviews, the call log). Everything there\n  is an `/api` route too, but those routes answer only the owner's key: a client key gets 403.\n\n## Manuals, playbooks and links\n\nSome upstreams say more than their tools do:\n\n- A **manual**: how the upstream wants its tools used. Read it once before its tools: `read_resource`\n  `gateway://u/<upstream>/manual` (MCP), or `GET /api/manuals/{name}`. `GET /api/manuals` lists them; MCP clients with\n  the Skills extension also get them as skills (`skill://<manual>/SKILL.md`). The gateway adds a short note at the\n  top saying how its tools and links are named here; the rest is as the upstream wrote it.\n- **Playbooks**: the upstream's step-by-step way to do one task (its MCP prompts). `search_tools` (and\n  `GET /api/tools?q=…`, in `playbooks`) suggests one when it fits; `get_playbook` or\n  `GET /api/playbooks/{name}/messages?topic=tides` fills it in, each argument its own query parameter as the\n  playbook names it (`GET /api/playbooks/{name}` lists them; `?arguments={…}`, a JSON object, works too). Named\n  `<upstream>-<prompt>`.\n- **Links** a tool returns, like `cookbook://recipes/123`: read them as they came with `read_resource` or\n  `GET /api/resources?uri=…`; the gateway finds the upstream by the scheme. `GET /api/links` lists the kinds.\n\nReading any of these uses the upstream's credential and is logged like a call, but needs no write scope.\n\n## Pack keys\n\nA key can be locked to a **pack**: a kit for one kind of work, curated by the owner. `GET /api/me` says so (`pack`,\nand `packs` when the key opens several). A pack is a small server of its own at one address, `/p/<pack>/`:\n\n- MCP at `/p/<pack>/mcp`, and REST under `/p/<pack>/api`: every `/api` route a key may use (health and sign-in stay\n  at the root), with links that stay under that address.\n- Its own guide at `/p/<pack>/llms.txt` and OpenAPI document at `/p/<pack>/openapi.json`, rendered at\n  `/p/<pack>/docs`. They describe the pack and nothing else: with a pack key, read them rather than this guide.\n- The older ways still work: MCP at `/mcp/p/<pack>`, and REST or `/mcp` naming the pack with the `x-gateway-pack`\n  header. Plain `/mcp` and `/api` serve the key's default pack.\n- A pack's address can change when its owner renames it: the old one keeps answering, and `GET /api/me` gives the\n  address it has now (`packs[].mcpUrl`).\n\nA pack key sees only the pack. Over MCP the server is named after the pack and its description opens the server\ninstructions. It has the gateway's shape: `search_tools`, `describe_tool`, `call_tool` and the others work over the\npack only, and every tool of the pack is listed directly, in the pack's own wording. A key the owner gave\npins of its own lists only those and finds the rest with `search_tools` (`PATCH /api/clients/{id}` with `pins`, by\nthe pack's names; `pins: null` lists the whole pack again). The pins set for every client never apply inside a pack.\n\nA pack sets some inputs of its tools. An input the pack fills itself is not in the tool's schema: leave it out, a\ncall that sends it is refused (400). An input the pack gives a default shows that default in the tool's schema; send\nyour own value to replace it.\n\nA pack can have a skill and playbooks written for it, by its owner, over its own tools:\n\n- Its **skill**, \"Working with <title>\": what the pack is for, which tool does which job, what is already set, and\n  what it can't do. Read it once, before the pack's tools: `read_resource` `skill://<pack>/SKILL.md` (MCP), or\n  `GET /api/resources?uri=skill://<pack>/SKILL.md`. `GET /api/me` gives the address (`pack.skill`, null when the\n  pack has none), the server instructions name it, and MCP clients with the Skills extension get it as a skill. It\n  also opens the pack's guide, `/p/<pack>/llms.txt`.\n- Its **own playbooks**: listed first in `GET /api/playbooks` and as prompts, with `upstream: null` and `pack` set,\n  under the name the pack gave them (`say-hello`, not `<upstream>-<prompt>`). `search_tools` suggests them, and\n  `get_playbook` or `GET /api/playbooks/{name}/messages?guest=Sam` (each argument its own query parameter) fills one\n  in with your arguments and the pack's own values. They belong to no connection: `?upstream=<id>` leaves them out,\n  and no connection counts them.\n\nPlaybooks, manuals and calls stay inside the pack too. A call to anything else is refused (403) and logged; the\nper-upstream passthrough (`/u/{id}/mcp`) is refused too. A pack key opens only the links its tools returned in the\nlast day (through `/u/{upstream}/r/…` and `read_resource`), so `GET /api/links` is empty for it. Its connections\n(`GET /api/upstreams`, `list_upstreams`) count only what the pack holds: its tools under the pack's names, its\nplaybooks and its manuals. A search that matches nothing in the pack says what the pack covers: `hint` on\n`GET /api/tools?q=…`, and over MCP `Nothing in <pack> matches \"<query>\". This pack covers: …`. Ask again by one of\nthose, or say it can't be done with this pack. Access can end: a 403 then says which pack, the day it ended and to\nask the owner for a new invite; over MCP the connection still opens and says the same. Once the owner has extended\nit, send a request that was refused with a new `Idempotency-Key`: the old one answers the refusal again for a day.\n\nA key that opens several packs has one connection per pack, and is refused (403) a pack it doesn't open with the\naddresses of those it does. An `Idempotency-Key` is remembered per key and route, not per pack: sent again with the\nsame body at another of the key's packs, it answers the first result again. So use a new `Idempotency-Key` for each\nnew request.\n\nThe owner's key at a pack's address (or with the header) is answered as a write key of that pack: what its agents\nsee. Routes that manage the gateway answer 403 there. From the root, `GET /api/tools?pack=<pack>` searches as the\npack's keys do, and with `as=<client id>` as that one key.\n\n## Writing a pack\n\nWith the owner's key, at the root (a pack's own address answers 403 to these), an agent can write a pack as the owner\ndoes in `/app`. Every save is checked first and refused whole when what it sends is wrong: a 400 whose `detail`\nstarts `Not saved:`, with one entry per problem under `errors`. Nothing is half saved.\n\n- **Variables**: named values of the pack. `PUT /api/packs/{name}/variables` with\n  `{\"variables\": [{\"name\": \"city\", \"value\": \"Lisbon\"}]}` replaces the whole set. A name is lowercase letters, digits\n  and `_`; a value is text, a number or true/false.\n- **Inputs**: `PATCH /api/packs/{name}/tools/{tool}` with `fixed` (the pack fills the input on every call, and its\n  keys never see it), `defaults` (shown with the pack's value, which the agent may replace) and `person` (hidden, and\n  filled with one variable, written `{{open}}city}}` and nothing else, as it is for whoever calls). An input in none\n  is the agent's. A value writes a variable as `{{open}}city}}`; a value that is one variable and nothing else keeps\n  the variable's type. `{tool}` is the name the pack calls the tool: its alias, else `<upstream>.<tool>`.\n- **Per person**: an input in `person` takes the person's own value, else the pack's, else nothing is sent and the\n  tool's own default applies. A variable only such inputs read may have `\"value\": null`. Set a person's values when\n  sharing (`values` on `POST /api/packs/{name}/people`) or later with `PUT /api/packs/{name}/people/{client}/values`;\n  each is checked against every input that reads it. A person's value is theirs to know, so an input that limits\n  what they reach (an account) belongs in `fixed`.\n- **A playbook**: `POST /api/packs/{name}/playbooks` with `{\"name\": \"find-roles\", \"markdown\": \"…\"}`;\n  `PUT` and `DELETE /api/packs/{name}/playbooks/{playbook}` replace and remove one. The Markdown opens with front\n  matter (`title`, `description`, `arguments`), then the steps:\n\n  ```markdown\n  ---\n  title: Find roles\n  description: Finds open roles in a city and lists the best five.\n  arguments:\n    - name: role\n      description: The kind of role, e.g. product designer.\n      required: true\n  ---\n\n  1. Run `jobs.search_postings` with `query` set to {{open}}role}}, in {{open}}city}}.\n  2. List the five best matches, newest first.\n  ```\n\n  The text writes an argument or one of the pack's variables as `{{open}}name}}` and numbers its steps. It names the\n  pack's tools in backticks, by the names the pack calls them: only a name in backticks is read as a tool.\n- **The skill**: `PUT /api/packs/{name}/skill` with `{\"markdown\": \"…\"}`. A pack has one, \"Working with <title>\", in\n  six `##` sections: What it is for, Which tool for which job, Values already set, Playbooks, Gotchas, What it can't\n  do. Send the sections only: the gateway writes the front matter and the heading from the pack.\n- **Try a draft**: `POST /api/packs/{name}/checks` with `{\"kind\": \"playbook\", \"markdown\": \"…\"}` (or\n  `\"kind\": \"skill\"`) answers `findings` and saves nothing.\n\nWhat the checks say:\n\n- Refused, as an `error`: a tool in backticks that is not in the pack, or written by its own name where the pack\n  gives it another; a `{{open}}word}}` in a playbook's text or the skill that is neither an argument nor a\n  variable; front matter that can't be read; a fixed or default value the tool's input schema does not take.\n- Saved, with a `warning` in `findings`: a tool a read-only key can't run, a name shaped like a tool that is none\n  (a file's name, like `tokens.css`, is not taken for one), a playbook past 12 steps, a section the skill leaves out,\n  a playbook the skill names that the pack lacks (in its Playbooks section, or anywhere as `<upstream>-<prompt>` of a\n  connection the pack's tools or playbooks come from).\n- In a fixed or default value, a `{{open}}word}}` the pack has no variable for is sent as written, with a warning:\n  a tool whose own input is a template keeps working. In a playbook's text or the skill nothing is sent as written,\n  so there it is an error. That holds inside fenced code blocks too: there is no escape to keep a `{{open}}word}}` as\n  written. A playbook's front matter (its title, description and arguments' descriptions) is not read for them: it is\n  served as written.\n\n`GET /api/packs/{name}` shows the pack as it stands: `findings`, `ownPlaybooks`, `skill`, and `flags` once a tool\nchanged under a text. A text is never blocked by a tool that moves: it is flagged, and saving it again clears the\nflag. The pack's keys get what you wrote at once (see Pack keys).\n\n## Rules worth knowing\n\n- Tool names are `<upstream>.<tool>`, e.g. `cookbook.research_recipes`.\n- Search returns only tools that clearly match what you asked. A question that matches nothing answers `count: 0`\n  (`No tools match.` over MCP): ask again in other words, and if it stays empty there is no tool for it here. A\n  gateway with fewer than 50 tools, or without search by meaning, has no such bar: it answers with its nearest tools\n  and every keyword hit, however weak.\n- A tool that changes things (`needsWrite: true`, `[changes things]` in MCP) needs a key with write scope; a\n  read-only key never sees those tools.\n- The owner can switch tools off: one tool, a group of a connection's tools, or everything on a connection that\n  changes or deletes things. An off tool is left out of lists and searches; calling it anyway is a 403 whose `detail`\n  says which switch is off. Ask the owner rather than looking for another way round.\n- Over MCP, a result bigger than your key's cap is cut: you get page 1 and a call id; `get_result` (or\n  `GET /api/calls/{id}/result?page=2`) gives the rest for an hour. REST answers are never cut.\n- `POST` runs accept `Idempotency-Key`: a retried request with the same key answers the first result again. A new\n  request needs a new key: the same one with another body is a 422.\n- Calls are rate-limited per key (`RateLimit` headers; 429 with `Retry-After` when over).\n- Errors are `application/problem+json` with a `detail` that says what to do next: arguments that don't match\n  the tool's input schema are a 400 listing each bad field in `errors`, before the upstream is called. A tool\n  that ran and reported its own failure answers 200 with `status: \"tool_error\"` and `result.isError: true`.\n- Links in a result that point at an upstream's own host are rewritten to `/u/{upstream}/r/<path>`: open them\n  with the same key (read-only, through the gateway).\n- A connection's tools come in groups (`groups` on `GET /api/upstreams`, printed by `list_upstreams`): narrow a\n  search to one with `group` and `upstream` (`search_tools`, or `GET /api/tools?upstream=…&group=…`).\n- In lists a tool has a short `summary`; `GET /api/tools/{name}` gives the full `description` and schemas.\n- Lists are HAL: follow `_links.next` until it is absent.\n- Send W3C trace context (a `traceparent` header on REST, `_meta.traceparent` on MCP) and each call is recorded as a\n  span inside your own trace; without it, each call is a trace of its own. Only metadata is traced, never arguments\n  or results.\n\n## Endpoints\n\n{{api.endpoints}}\n\n## MCP tools\n\n{{mcp.tools}}\n",
}
